# FormControl, FormGroup, FormArray — and When You Need FormArray

## 1. What is it?

These are the three building blocks of Angular's **Reactive Forms** — a tree of objects that models your form's state in TypeScript instead of scattering `ngModel` bindings across a template.

- **`FormControl`** — a single input's state: its value, validity, dirty/touched flags, and error object. One control per one field.
- **`FormGroup`** — a fixed, named collection of controls (and/or nested groups/arrays), keyed by string, like `{ name, email, address }`. Its own value/validity is the aggregate of its children.
- **`FormArray`** — an ordered, *variable-length* list of controls (or groups/arrays), indexed by position instead of by name. Same aggregate-validity behavior as `FormGroup`, but built for a list whose length changes at runtime.

All three extend the same base class (`AbstractControl`), which is why `.value`, `.valid`, `.invalid`, `.dirty`, `.touched`, `.valueChanges`, and `.statusChanges` work identically on any of them, and why they nest inside each other arbitrarily deep.

## 2. Why was it introduced?

Template-driven forms (`ngModel` everywhere) work fine for a handful of static fields, but two things break down as forms get more realistic:

- **You can't easily model a list whose length isn't known ahead of time** — invoice line items, phone numbers, team members, tags. With template-driven forms you're stuck manually managing an array of data and hoping `ngFor` + `ngModel` keeps everything in sync; there's no first-class "add/remove a control" API.
- **Validating and reading form state becomes awkward in a template-only world** — checking "is this nested section valid," computing a derived value across several fields, or unit-testing form logic without spinning up the DOM all get harder the more free-form the template's control structure is.

Reactive Forms solve this by making the form a plain object graph you build and query in the component class. `FormGroup` gives you a fixed-shape object; `FormArray` gives you the missing piece — a *resizable* collection — with `push()`, `insert()`, `removeAt()`, and `at()` as first-class operations, so "add a row" is one method call instead of hand-rolled array bookkeeping synced against the DOM.

## 3. How does it work?

**`FormControl`**
```ts
name = new FormControl('', Validators.required);
name.value;        // current value
name.valid;         // boolean
name.errors;        // { required: true } | null
name.valueChanges;  // Observable<string>
```

**`FormGroup`** — children accessed by key:
```ts
form = new FormGroup({
  name: new FormControl('', Validators.required),
  email: new FormControl('', [Validators.required, Validators.email])
});

form.get('email');   // the FormControl
form.value;          // { name: '...', email: '...' }
form.valid;           // false if ANY child is invalid
```

**`FormArray`** — children accessed by index, and the collection itself can grow/shrink:
```ts
items = new FormArray<FormGroup>([]);

items.push(newGroup);     // add to the end
items.insert(0, newGroup); // add at a specific index
items.removeAt(2);         // remove by index
items.at(0);                // get a control by index
items.length;               // current size
items.value;                 // array of each child's value
```

Key mechanics that matter in an interview:

- **Aggregate validity propagates upward.** If any control deep in a `FormGroup`/`FormArray` tree is invalid, every ancestor up to the root reports `invalid` too. You don't have to manually AND together child validities.
- **`FormBuilder` (`fb.group()`, `fb.array()`, `fb.control()`)** is just a thinner syntax for constructing the same tree — most real code uses it instead of `new FormGroup(...)` directly.
- **Validators are attached per-control**, not per-array — a `FormArray` itself can have array-level validators too (e.g., "at least one item"), separate from each row's own validators.
- **The template binds to structure, not just values** — `formGroupName`/`formArrayName`/`formControlName` directives walk the same tree you built in the class, which is what lets `@for` iterate `array.controls` and bind each one to its own `[formGroupName]="i"`.
- **`valueChanges`/`statusChanges` are Observables**, so they compose naturally with RxJS — and, in a modern signals-based app, are commonly bridged into a signal at the UI boundary with `toSignal()`, exactly like any other async source.

## 4. When do you need FormArray?

Whenever the **number of controls isn't fixed at compile time** — it depends on data loaded from the server or on user actions like "add another one":

- Invoice/order line items, cart items
- A list of phone numbers, addresses, or emergency contacts
- Dynamically added tags/skills/attachments
- A checklist whose items come from an API response

If your form's shape is always the same set of named fields, you just need a `FormGroup`. The moment the UI needs an "Add row" / "Remove row" button, you need a `FormArray`.

## 5. Example

An invoice form: a `FormGroup` for the top-level fields, containing a `FormArray` of `FormGroup`s — one per line item (product/qty/price) — with per-row validation and a running total.

**StackBlitz (compiles and renders correctly; seeded automatically on load with 2 valid rows + 1 intentionally invalid row so validation is visible immediately, no clicks required to verify):**
https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-n7sbjnaw

```ts
// app.component.ts
import { Component, OnInit, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import {
  FormArray,
  FormBuilder,
  FormGroup,
  ReactiveFormsModule,
  Validators
} from '@angular/forms';
import { toSignal } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { map, startWith } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule, ReactiveFormsModule],
  templateUrl: './app.component.html'
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {
  private fb = inject(FormBuilder);

  invoiceForm = this.fb.group({
    customerName: ['', Validators.required],
    items: this.fb.array<FormGroup>([])
  });

  totalValue = toSignal(
    this.invoiceForm.valueChanges.pipe(
      startWith(this.invoiceForm.getRawValue()),
      map(value =>
        (value.items ?? []).reduce(
          (sum, item: any) => sum + (Number(item.qty) || 0) * (Number(item.price) || 0),
          0
        )
      )
    ),
    { initialValue: 0 }
  );

  get items(): FormArray<FormGroup> {
    return this.invoiceForm.get('items') as FormArray<FormGroup>;
  }

  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.invoiceForm.patchValue({ customerName: 'Ada Lovelace' });
    this.addItem('Widget', 2, 9.99);
    this.addItem('Gadget', 1, 24.5);
    this.addItem('Mystery Item', 0, 5); // intentionally invalid: qty must be >= 1
  }

  addItem(product = '', qty = 1, price = 0): void {
    this.items.push(
      this.fb.group({
        product: [product, Validators.required],
        qty: [qty, [Validators.required, Validators.min(1)]],
        price: [price, [Validators.required, Validators.min(0)]]
      })
    );
  }

  removeItem(index: number): void {
    this.items.removeAt(index);
  }
}
```

```html
<!-- app.component.html -->
<div class="page">
  <h1>Invoice — Dynamic Line Items</h1>
  <p>A <code>FormArray</code> of <code>FormGroup</code>s, seeded automatically on load with two valid rows and one intentionally invalid row (qty 0) so validation is visible immediately.</p>

  <form [formGroup]="invoiceForm">
    <label>
      Customer name
      <input formControlName="customerName" />
      @if (invoiceForm.get('customerName')?.invalid && invoiceForm.get('customerName')?.touched) {
        <span class="error">Required</span>
      }
    </label>

    <div formArrayName="items">
      @for (item of items.controls; track item; let i = $index) {
        <div [formGroupName]="i" class="row" [class.invalid-row]="item.invalid">
          <label>
            Product
            <input formControlName="product" />
          </label>
          <label>
            Qty
            <input formControlName="qty" type="number" />
          </label>
          <label>
            Price
            <input formControlName="price" type="number" />
          </label>
          <button type="button" class="remove" (click)="removeItem(i)">Remove</button>
          @if (item.invalid) {
            <span class="error">Invalid row (check qty/price)</span>
          }
        </div>
      } @empty {
        <p>No line items yet.</p>
      }
    </div>

    <button type="button" (click)="addItem()">+ Add row</button>

    <div class="summary">
      <div>Rows: {{ items.length }}</div>
      <div>Total: {{ totalValue() | currency }}</div>
      <div>Form valid: {{ invoiceForm.valid }}</div>
    </div>
  </form>
</div>
```

